WEBVTT

00:00.690 --> 00:07.040
So I have the function g
of x is equal to 9 times 8

00:07.040 --> 00:09.400
to the x minus 1 power.

00:09.400 --> 00:16.070
And it's defined for
x being a positive--

00:16.070 --> 00:19.950
or if x is a positive-- integer.

00:19.950 --> 00:27.000
If x is a positive integer.

00:27.000 --> 00:29.000
So we could say the
domain of this function,

00:29.000 --> 00:32.080
or all the valid inputs
here are positive integers.

00:32.080 --> 00:34.970
So 1, 2, 3, 4, 5,
on and on and on.

00:34.970 --> 00:37.360
So this is an explicitly
defined function.

00:37.360 --> 00:41.370
What I now want to do is to
write a recursive definition

00:41.370 --> 00:42.990
of this exact same function.

00:42.990 --> 00:46.110
That given an x, it'll give
the exact same outputs.

00:46.110 --> 00:48.410
So let's first just try
to understand the inputs

00:48.410 --> 00:49.650
and outputs here.

00:49.650 --> 00:51.340
So let's make a little table.

00:51.340 --> 00:53.560
Let's make a table here.

00:53.560 --> 00:56.620
And let's think
about what happens

00:56.620 --> 01:01.880
when we put in various x's
into this function definition.

01:01.880 --> 01:03.780
So the domain is
positive integers.

01:03.780 --> 01:05.030
So let's try a couple of them.

01:05.030 --> 01:08.240
1, 2, 3, 4.

01:08.240 --> 01:11.410
And then see what the
corresponding g of x is.

01:11.410 --> 01:14.100
g of x.

01:14.100 --> 01:17.080
So when x is equal
to 1, g of x is

01:17.080 --> 01:19.780
9 times 8 to the
1 minus 1 power,

01:19.780 --> 01:23.310
9 times 8 to the 0
power, or 9 times 1.

01:23.310 --> 01:27.050
So g of x is going to be just 9.

01:27.050 --> 01:32.610
When x is 2, what's
going to happen?

01:32.610 --> 01:35.830
It'll be 9 times 8
to the 2 minus 1.

01:35.830 --> 01:39.010
So that's the same thing as
9 times 8 to the 1st power.

01:39.010 --> 01:40.600
And that's just going
to be 9 times 8.

01:40.600 --> 01:43.135
So that is 72.

01:43.135 --> 01:44.760
Actually let me just
write it that way.

01:44.760 --> 01:47.260
Let me write it
as just 9 times 8.

01:47.260 --> 01:49.270
9 times 8.

01:49.270 --> 01:52.980
Then when x is equal to
3, what's going on here?

01:52.980 --> 01:55.120
Well this is going
to be 3 minus 1 is 2.

01:55.120 --> 01:56.690
So it's going to be 8 squared.

01:56.690 --> 01:59.380
So it's going to be
9 times 8 squared.

01:59.380 --> 02:04.290
So we could write that
as 9 times 8 times 8.

02:04.290 --> 02:06.650
I think you see a little
bit of a pattern forming.

02:06.650 --> 02:10.525
When x is 4, this is going to be
8 to the 4 minus 1 power, or 9

02:10.525 --> 02:11.950
to the 3rd power.

02:11.950 --> 02:19.090
So that's 9 times
8 times 8 times 8.

02:19.090 --> 02:20.726
So this gives a
good clue about how

02:20.726 --> 02:22.100
we would define
this recursively.

02:22.100 --> 02:25.670
Notice, if our first term,
when x equals 1 is 9,

02:25.670 --> 02:31.310
every term after that is 8
times the preceding term.

02:31.310 --> 02:33.850
Is 8 times the preceding term.

02:33.850 --> 02:35.700
8 times the preceding term.

02:35.700 --> 02:37.700
8 times the preceding term.

02:37.700 --> 02:39.730
So let's define that as
a recursive function.

02:39.730 --> 02:41.960
So first define our base case.

02:41.960 --> 02:45.560
So we could say
g of x-- and I'll

02:45.560 --> 02:48.670
do this is a new color
because I'm overusing the red.

02:48.670 --> 02:49.980
I like the blue.

02:49.980 --> 02:51.620
g of x.

02:51.620 --> 02:53.550
Well we can define
our base case.

02:53.550 --> 02:57.570
It's going to be equal
to 9 if x is equal to 1.

02:57.570 --> 03:04.580
g of x equals 9 if x equals 1.

03:04.580 --> 03:07.170
So that took care of
that right over there.

03:07.170 --> 03:11.140
And then if it
equals anything else

03:11.140 --> 03:13.570
it equals the previous g of x.

03:13.570 --> 03:17.760
So if we're looking at--
let's go all the way down

03:17.760 --> 03:21.410
to x minus 1, and then an x.

03:21.410 --> 03:33.120
So if this entry right over
here is g of x minus 1,

03:33.120 --> 03:35.209
however many times
you multiply the 8s

03:35.209 --> 03:38.230
and we have a 9 in front,
so this is g of x minus 1.

03:38.230 --> 03:42.360
We know that g of x-- we know
that this one right over here

03:42.360 --> 03:47.460
is going to be the previous
entry, g of x minus 1.

03:47.460 --> 03:55.794
The previous entry times 8.

03:55.794 --> 03:57.210
So we could write
that right here.

03:57.210 --> 03:59.390
Times 8.

03:59.390 --> 04:02.290
So for any other
x other than 1, g

04:02.290 --> 04:07.060
of x is equal to the previous
entry-- so it's g of-- I'll

04:07.060 --> 04:16.190
do that in a blue color--
g of x minus 1 times 8.

04:16.190 --> 04:29.190
If x is greater than 1, or
x is integer greater than 1.

04:29.190 --> 04:32.570
Now let's verify that
this actually works.

04:32.570 --> 04:35.070
So let's draw
another table here.

04:38.210 --> 04:41.230
So once again, we're
going to have x

04:41.230 --> 04:42.864
and we're going to have g of x.

04:42.864 --> 04:45.280
But this time we're going to
use this recursive definition

04:45.280 --> 04:45.880
for g of x.

04:45.880 --> 04:47.255
And the reason
why it's recursive

04:47.255 --> 04:49.390
is it's referring to itself.

04:49.390 --> 04:52.580
In its own definition, it's
saying hey, g of x, well if x

04:52.580 --> 04:54.580
doesn't equal 1 it's going
to be g of x minus 1.

04:54.580 --> 04:56.080
It's using the function itself.

04:56.080 --> 04:58.666
But we'll see that it
actually does work out.

04:58.666 --> 04:59.290
So let's see...

04:59.290 --> 05:04.790
When x is equal to 1, so g
of 1-- well if x equals 1,

05:04.790 --> 05:06.380
it's equal to 9.

05:06.380 --> 05:07.140
It's equal to 9.

05:07.140 --> 05:09.150
So that was pretty
straightforward.

05:09.150 --> 05:11.380
What happens when x equals 2?

05:11.380 --> 05:14.400
Well when x equals 2, this
case doesn't apply anymore.

05:14.400 --> 05:15.850
We go down to this case.

05:15.850 --> 05:17.840
So when x is equal
to 2 it's going

05:17.840 --> 05:20.750
to be equivalent
to g of 2 minus 1.

05:20.750 --> 05:22.250
Let me write this down.

05:22.250 --> 05:28.770
It's going to be equivalent
to g of 2 minus 1 times 8,

05:28.770 --> 05:33.590
which is the same thing
as g of 1 times 8.

05:33.590 --> 05:34.670
And what's g of 1?

05:34.670 --> 05:36.890
Well g of 1 is right over here.

05:36.890 --> 05:38.010
g of 1 is 9.

05:38.010 --> 05:40.820
So this is going to
be equal to 9 times 8.

05:40.820 --> 05:42.730
Exactly what we got over here.

05:42.730 --> 05:45.840
And of course this was
equivalent to g of 2.

05:45.840 --> 05:47.060
So let me write this.

05:47.060 --> 05:49.370
This is g of 2.

05:49.370 --> 05:50.870
Let me scroll over
a little bit so I

05:50.870 --> 05:52.710
don't get all scrunched up.

05:52.710 --> 05:54.780
So now let's go to 3.

05:54.780 --> 05:56.110
Let's go to 3.

05:56.110 --> 05:57.890
And right now I'll
write g of 3 first.

05:57.890 --> 06:01.650
So g of 3 is equal
to-- we're going

06:01.650 --> 06:09.130
to this case-- it's equal
to g of 3 minus 1 times 8.

06:09.130 --> 06:13.770
So that's equal
to g of 2 times 8.

06:13.770 --> 06:15.060
Well what's g of 2?

06:15.060 --> 06:18.060
Well g of 2, we already
figured out is 9 times 8.

06:18.060 --> 06:22.400
So it's equal to 9 times 8--
that's g of 2-- times 8 again.

06:22.400 --> 06:24.970
And so you see we get
the exact same results.

06:24.970 --> 06:29.690
So this is the recursive
definition of this function.
